  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nAs of roughly 19Z this afternoon, some scattered light rain showers \nare moving over the forecast area. Expectations over the next few \nhours are that these rain showers will maintain their current \nstrength as they move eastward, producing light rain. No severe \nweather is expected with these showers, although can't quite rule \nout a stray pulse of lightning or two in the strongest showers. \n\nThis evening, an upper-level shortwave along with a developing low-\nlevel jet will move over the region, helping to develop the next \nround of showers and storms. Expectations for tonight are that \nstorms will develop over central South Dakota this evening and \nprogress eastward through the overnight period. There is also some \nsignal in CAMs showing additional develop in North Dakota late this \nevening that will drop south into northeastern South Dakota and \nwestern Minnesota. Comparing the latest CAM guidance, it seems that \nindividual models show a signal for storms in either central or \nnortheastern South Dakota, but very few show both. Ensembles trend \ntowards the central South Dakota scenario, and for that reason \nforecaster confidence is a bit higher in that area seeing severe \nweather tonight. Currently, a Marginal Risk for severe weather is in \neffect for the entire forecast area tonight. \n\nHigh-resolution ensemble models indicate that the hail threat today \nwill most likely be confined to central South Dakota. There, MUCAPE \nof 1000-2000 J/kg is expected, along with shear of 30-40 knots and \nmid-level lapse rates between 7 and 8 C/km providing additional \nsupport for the hail threat. As storms progress beyond central South \nDakota, the signal in the Updraft Helicity tracks drops off \nsignificantly, thereby reducing the hail potential moving into the \novernight period. Wind will also be a severe threat from these \nstorms, and likely becomes the dominant threat as storms progress \ninto northeastern South Dakota. However, there is still some \nsignificant uncertainty as to how exactly storms will evolve during \nthe overnight period and if storms will maintain their strength as \nthe threat moves east. \n\nAnother shortwave aloft and associated vorticity maximum is set to \nmove into the region Saturday evening, thereby creating another \nchance for storm development. North central South Dakota is the main \narea of concern on Saturday, and a Slight Risk has been issued. \nStorms will once again be expected during the evening to overnight \ntime frame, although there is still a bit of uncertainty on how far \nsouth storms will reach during the overnight period. For storms that \ndo develop over and/or move into northern South Dakota, highest \nconfidence is currently in the potential hail threat. Similar MUCAPE \nvalues of 1000-2000 J/kg are expected, with perhaps a slight \nincrease in shear from today's setup to 40-50 knots. A bit less \nconfidence in the wind threat currently, but with pockets of 1000 \nJ/kg of DCAPE potentially present along with some CAMs resolving a \nquasi-linear storm mode, severe wind will likely also be a threat. \n\nStarting Sunday and into next week, the broad upper-level ridge that \nhas been in place will shift eastward. As a result multiple upper-\nlevel jet streaks are set to pass over the region through early next \nweek. This setup will be conducive to additional rain chances, with \nthe best chances generally located along the North Dakota/South \nDakota border. On Sunday in particular, another Marginal Risk for \nsevere weather is in place. Beyond that point, confidence drops in \nthe severe weather potential over the Aberdeen forecast area.",
